, indices, state)` â€” if no 710 is in play and evolving into 710 is offered, pick it; otherwise the unchanged strongest-resulting-card ranking.\n- `_engine_evolve_override(obs)` â€” decisive MAIN-phase form (see below).\n\n**Wired into three evolve decision points:**\n1. `_choose_main` step 2 (greedy ladder / rollout tails) â†’ uses `_prioritized_evolve_index`.\n2. `_choose_evolve` (forced `SelectType.EVOLVE` selects) â†’ uses `_prioritized_evolve_index`.\n3. `_agent_impl` MAIN branch â†’ calls `_engine_evolve_override` **before** search.\n\nThe override matters because `SEARCH_MAIN=True` picks the top-level MAIN action by rollout eval, bypassing the greedy evolve ladder â€” so a greedy-only change would not affect the real decision. The override forces evolving into 710 when none is in play, **except** when a lethal attack is available (it never skips a KO to set up the engine). Once a 710 is in play, all paths revert to the original ranking, so competing Mega Meganium endpoints are pursued exactly as before â€” matching the hypothesis's \"preserve evolution ranking after the energy engine exists.\"\n\nBehavior is idempotent and loop-safe: after one line evolves into 710, `_wild_growth_in_play` returns true, the override yields `None`, and a second Chikorita line proceeds to its Mega endpoint normally.\n\n## Concerns for the human reviewer\n\n- **Card-ID assumptions unverified at edit time.** I could not load the `cg` card DB in this workspace, so I trusted the hypothesis that 710 is the Wild Growth Meganium and that the deck's `EVOLVE` options expose the resulting card via `opt.cardId`. Please confirm `opt.cardId` on an `OptionType.EVOLVE` option is the *target* evolution card (the existing `_best_card_option` already relies on this) and that 710's Ability is the Grass-doubling engine.\n- **Lethal-only guard.** The override defers only for an outright lethal attack, not for other high-value non-lethal lines (e.g. a big non-KO attack). This is intentional to keep the mechanism narrow, but it means on the evolve turn we may forgo a strong attack to establish the engine â€” the exact tradeoff under test. Worth watching cross-deck for tempo loss.\n- **Mega ends turn.** Since Mega evolution ends the turn, forcing 710 first also preserves the ability to keep acting that turn; confirm this doesn't interact oddly with the search rollout's turn-boundary detection (it shouldn't â€” the override returns before search).\n\nI did not run benchmarks or games, per the contract; the controller performs validation and matches after exit.","ttft_ms":2459,"ttft_stream_ms":1498,"time_to_request_ms":246,"type":"result","duration_ms":196140,"uuid":"979b3d59-c69f-4ff6-8bef-1bb7b5dbd373"}
